      {
        "id": "thm-same-sample-control-coefficient-remainder-is-op-one-over-n",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Same-sample coefficient remainder is of order $O_P(n^{-1})$",
        "deps": [
          "def-control-variate-estimator",
          "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "thm-factorization-of-expectations-for-independent-variables",
          "thm-change-of-variables-for-expectation",
          "thm-kolmogorov-iid-l1-strong-law",
          "cor-chebyshev-inequality-for-random-variables",
          "cor-cauchy-schwarz-for-random-variables",
          "lem-measurable-functions-preserve-independence",
          "thm-arithmetic-and-lattice-operations-preserve-measurability"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "statement": "Use the notation $Pg=\\int g\\,dP$ from [[def-control-variate-estimator]]. Let $q\\ge1$ be fixed and let $(X_i)_{i\\ge1}$ be an IID sequence on $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ with law $P$. For each $n\\ge1$, assume $P|f|^4<\\infty$ and $P|h_j|^4<\\infty$ for each $j$. Set $\\theta=Ph$, define $\\Sigma_{jk}=E_P[(h_j-\\theta_j)(h_k-\\theta_k)]$ and $c_j=E_P[(h_j-\\theta_j)(f-Pf)]$, and suppose $\\Sigma$ is positive definite, meaning $v^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma v>0$ for every nonzero vector $v$. Put $\\beta^*=\\Sigma^{-1}c$. Define $\\bar f_n=n^{-1}\\sum_i f(X_i)$, $\\bar h_n=n^{-1}\\sum_i h(X_i)$,\n\n$$\\widehat\\Sigma_n=\\frac1n\\sum_i(h(X_i)-\\bar h_n)(h(X_i)-\\bar h_n)^{\\mathsf T},\\qquad \\widehat c_n=\\frac1n\\sum_i(h(X_i)-\\bar h_n)(f(X_i)-\\bar f_n).$$\n\nand let $\\widehat\\beta_n=\\widehat\\Sigma_n^{-1}\\widehat c_n$ when $\\widehat\\Sigma_n$ is invertible and $0$ otherwise. Define the plug-in estimator pointwise by $\\widehat\\mu_{\\widehat\\beta_n}:=\\bar f_n-\\widehat\\beta_n^{\\mathsf T}(\\bar h_n-\\theta)$. For a scalar random sequence $R_n$, write $R_n=O_P(n^{-1})$ when $nR_n$ is bounded in probability: for each $\\varepsilon>0$ some finite $M$ and $N$ satisfy $\\mathbb P(|nR_n|>M)<\\varepsilon$ for all $n\\ge N$. For a fixed-dimensional vector or matrix, $O_P(a_n)$ means its Euclidean or Frobenius norm divided by $a_n$ is bounded in probability. Then\n\n$$\\widehat\\mu_{\\widehat\\beta_n}-\\widehat\\mu_{\\beta^*}=-(\\widehat\\beta_n-\\beta^*)^{\\mathsf T}(\\bar h_n-\\theta)=O_P(n^{-1}).$$\n\nThis result bounds only the same-sample coefficient-times-control-mean remainder; it does not assert that $\\widehat\\mu_{\\widehat\\beta_n}$ is unbiased."
      },
